CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a perspective line drawing of a rectangular coil rotating in a uniform magnetic field. The coil appears as a tall rectangular loop of several stacked turns, rendered as a striped gray band so the windings show, and tilted so its face is seen obliquely. A vertical axis labeled z passes through the coil's center, solid above and below the loop and dashed where it crosses the interior; a small circular arrow curls around its top to give the sense of rotation. A second axis labeled x runs diagonally toward the lower left, with a dashed segment continuing to the upper right. From the center point, a bold blue arrow labeled B points horizontally to the right as the uniform field, while an orange arrow labeled n-hat, the unit normal to the coil's surface, springs from the same origin and tilts down and to the right; a thin arc between the two marks the angle theta. Anchoring n-hat and B at a shared origin makes the angle between them the one quantity the reader tracks as the loop turns. The arrangement sets up the flux through a rotating coil and the emf that rotation induces.

PROJECTION TYPE: perspective
Use the scaffold's true perspective projection after building all geometry. Use this only when the source has real vanishing-point convergence or distance-based size shrinkage; then depth should visibly affect apparent size.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - tall rectangular coil loop drawn as a striped gray band of several stacked turns (multi-turn winding), face seen obliquely
  - vertical z-axis line through the coil center, solid above and below the loop, dashed where it passes behind/through the loop interior
  - 'z' label at top of vertical axis
  - small circular arrow curling around the top of the z-axis indicating rotation sense
  - diagonal x-axis running to lower-left with arrowhead, plus dashed continuation to upper-right through the loop
  - 'x' label at lower-left end of the diagonal axis
  - short horizontal y-axis segment extending to the left of the origin
  - origin point at the center of the coil where all vectors are anchored
  - bold blue arrow pointing horizontally to the right from the origin, labeled 'B' with vector overbar
  - orange arrow from the origin tilting down and to the right, the unit normal, labeled 'n̂'
  - thin arc between the blue B arrow and the orange n̂ arrow with 'θ' label
  - flux/emf readout text (Φ = BA cos θ, ε = NBAω sin θ)
  - graph panel (alternate view): synchronized Φ(t) and ε(t) sinusoidal traces with time axis and moving playhead marker

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World scale: coil loop is a rectangle 2.0 wide × 3.0 tall (outer), drawn as a flat band of thickness 0.10 with 4–5 visible stacked turns (stripes offset ~0.03 along the coil normal), band width 0.10. Coil is centered on the origin and pivots about the vertical z-axis; at θ = 0 the coil plane contains z and the x-axis (plane normal along +y). Axes: z vertical from -3.2 to +3.4 (solid outside |z|>1.5, dashed inside the loop interior), label 'z' at top; x-axis runs toward the viewer's lower-left from origin to length 3.2 with arrowhead and 'x' label, and continues as a dashed ray to the upper-right for 2.2 units; y-axis shown only as a short solid horizontal segment 1.2 units to the LEFT of the origin (screen-left). Rotation arrow: small ellipse of radius 0.45 around the z-axis at z ≈ 2.6 with an arrowhead giving the spin sense. B vector: bold blue arrow from origin along +y (screen-right), length 2.2, shaft thickness ~0.07, label '𝐁' with overbar at its tip. n̂ vector: orange arrow from the origin, length 1.3, lying in the horizontal xy-plane but drawn in this view as tilting down and to the right; direction n̂ = (−sin θ·x̂ + cos θ·ŷ) so θ is measured from B. Theta arc: thin black arc of radius 0.55 spanning from the B direction to n̂, with 'θ' label just outside its midpoint. Colors: coil band gray #9E9E9E with lighter #C9C9C9 highlight stripes and thin dark outlines #555; axes and labels black #000; B arrow blue #1F6FD0; n̂ arrow and its label orange #F07C1E. Physics: Φ = B·A·cos θ with A = 2.0 × 3.0 (use B = 1 T, A = 1 m², N = 1 for readouts unless labeled otherwise); ε = N·B·A·ω·sin θ with θ = ωt, ω default such that one revolution ≈ 6 s. Graph view: two stacked plots over two full periods, Φ (blue) as cosine, ε (orange) as sine, shared time axis, vertical playhead line tied to the current θ.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "thetaAngle",
    "type": "slider",
    "teaches": "Only the component of B along the coil normal contributes to flux: as θ grows the orange n̂ swings away from B and the flux readout falls as cos θ, vanishing when the coil face is edge-on to the field.",
    "label": "Coil angle θ (degrees)",
    "range": [
      0,
      360,
      1
    ],
    "default": 35
  },
  {
    "id": "spin",
    "type": "animation",
    "teaches": "Steady rotation makes θ increase linearly in time, so the flux varies sinusoidally and the induced emf — its rate of change — is largest exactly when the flux passes through zero.",
    "label": "Rotate coil at constant ω",
    "loop": true
  },
  {
    "id": "viewMode",
    "type": "toggle",
    "teaches": "Links the geometric angle between n̂ and B in the 3D picture to the sinusoidal Φ(t) and ε(t) curves used in the AC-generator calculation.",
    "label": "View",
    "options": [
      "3D vectors",
      "Flux & emf graphs",
      "Both"
    ],
    "default": "3D vectors"
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "Confirms that n̂ is genuinely perpendicular to the coil plane and that both n̂ and B lie in the horizontal plane while the rotation axis stays vertical."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Face-on: maximum flux",
    "narration": "At θ = 0 the orange normal n̂ points straight along B, the coil face is broadside to the field, and the flux reaches its maximum BA.",
    "state": {
      "thetaAngle": 0,
      "viewMode": "3D vectors"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Tilted coil",
    "narration": "Drag θ to 35°: only the component of B along n̂ threads the loop, so the flux drops to BA cos 35°.",
    "state": {
      "thetaAngle": 35,
      "viewMode": "3D vectors"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Edge-on: zero flux",
    "narration": "At θ = 90° n̂ is perpendicular to B, field lines skim past the coil plane and the flux is zero — yet this is where the flux is changing fastest.",
    "state": {
      "thetaAngle": 90,
      "viewMode": "3D vectors"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Read the curves",
    "narration": "Switch to the graph view: Φ traces BA cos θ while ε traces NBAω sin θ, a quarter cycle out of step.",
    "state": {
      "thetaAngle": 90,
      "viewMode": "Flux & emf graphs"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Continuous rotation",
    "narration": "Play the steady spin with both panels shown — the turning angle between n̂ and B is what generates the alternating emf.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "Both"
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "azimuthDeg": 45,
  "elevationDeg": 18,
  "rollDeg": 0,
  "heightFraction": 0.7
}

NOTES: Use PerspectiveCamera (fov ~35) with OrbitControls, target at the origin. Build the multi-turn coil from 4–5 concentric extruded rectangle outlines (THREE.Shape with a hole, or TubeGeometry along a rounded-rectangle path) grouped and offset along the local normal, then rotate the whole group about the world Y (up = z) axis by θ so the coil, n̂, and the theta arc stay rigidly linked. Dashed axis segments via LineDashedMaterial with computeLineDistances(). Vectors as ArrowHelper or custom cylinder+cone meshes so the blue B arrow can be drawn noticeably thicker. Theta arc as a Line built from a small arc of points in the horizontal plane, regenerated each frame. Labels (z, x, B, n̂, θ, flux readout) as CSS2DRenderer/sprite overlays so they stay screen-facing and legible. Graph view rendered in a 2D canvas or SVG overlay panel driven by the same θ state; in 'Both' mode dock it to the right third of the frame and shrink the 3D viewport rather than moving the camera. Animation drives θ with a clock delta and simply writes back into the slider value so slider and animation share one state variable.

Follow the interaction plan above.

